WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
This is a walkthrough of an independent concept
by Mudasir Khan.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It is not affiliated with Tesla,
and Tesla has not endorsed or funded it.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Here is how one Cybercab branch would go
from a physical site, to a vetted owner, to launch and scale.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The vehicle is Cybercab only. It has no steering wheel
or pedals, so this is booked autonomous mobility,
not a self-drive rental counter.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:31.000
The proposal is for riders to book through Tesla's Robotaxi app,
but that would need Tesla's agreement and is not confirmed.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.800
The branch's job is ground operations: clean, charge,
inspect, and stage each vehicle for its next trip.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.500
Step one is the site. A branch is a parking lot,
a small office, a wash bay, and charging stalls.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.500
Before a lease, the site has to clear zoning,
have enough electrical capacity, and hold wash
and wastewater rights.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.500
Ready-to-use premises are preferred
because they are faster.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:04.000
Step two is the owner. The proposal includes
a first-time entrepreneur owner grant.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
On the application, owners initial the fill-to-200
and rural-first rules.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Reviewers score each line from 1 to 5:
first-time owner, rural commitment, operations learning,
working capital, background and brand safety,
a training plan, and no guaranteed income.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The decision is advance, hold, or decline.
This is a proposed program, not an existing Tesla grant.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.500
Acceptance and milestone funding are not guaranteed.
Amounts depend on a pilot term sheet and legal review.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.000
Step three is the gates.
Nothing launches until every gate clears.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:50.000
That means a lease, zoning, a wash permit, enough power,
and a path to 200 stalls.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.600
It also needs written vehicle access from Tesla,
local operating permission, insurance, financing,
and payment terms. All unresolved today.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:06.000
Step four is staff and systems.
Shift leads open and close the lot and count every unit:
ready, charging, wash, quarantine, or maintenance.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Cleaners run the wash and photo inspections.
Technicians watch charging and lot safety.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:25.300
In the intended autonomous model, staff do not drive the cabs.
Written procedures cover opening, closing, washing,
charging, inspection, and incidents.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:33.000
Step five is launch. The conditional target is about
seven weeks to open the first branch and begin scaling.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Site, hiring, and drills run in parallel.
Weeks five to seven stop without vehicle access,
insurance, or operating permission in writing.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.900
If those approvals slip, the date moves.
It is not a finished national network.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:55.000
Step six is measurement. The weekly report tracks
utilization, service quality, and finances.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Traffic impact is a hypothesis, not a promise.
Pilots would measure total vehicle miles,
including empty trips, plus peak load and occupancy.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.400
Empty miles could make traffic worse,
which is why they get measured.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:15.000
Step seven is the fill rule.
A branch grows until it holds 200 Cybercabs on site.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.700
Only then does the next branch open.
That keeps utilization honest and avoids empty lots.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:28.000
Step eight is rollout. The proposed pilots are
the Hartford suburbs in Connecticut
and suburban New Jersey.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:34.000
These are suburban pilots, not rural ones,
and no sites have been approved.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.900
After the pilots, the long-term ambition is rural first,
where coverage is thinnest, then denser places,
and cities last.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:50.000
So: site, owner vetting, gates, staff, and launch.
Then pilot, measure, fill to 200, and only then expand
under rural-first, cities-last.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:58.000
This is an independent concept by Mudasir Khan,
not affiliated with Tesla.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:06.540
Vehicle supply, financing, insurance, permits,
and territory approvals are all still unresolved.
